window.STORY_TRANSCRIPTS={"0":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Mike","Hi Christine, it is Mike, uh, the one that's your brother, and I am here to tell you a little bit about the Christmas present that you are getting from Mike, the one that's your husband. Uh, and that is that we are heading out tomorrow for a little bit. We're leaving you guys to go see our wonderful grandparents, and Mike is going to set up all of his crazy audio recording equipment And we are just going to have a conversation with them and get them telling some of our favorite stories and maybe some that we don't even know yet. And just get all those memories recorded straight from the horse's mouth. And then Mike is going to produce it and make a sweet audio file for you with those memories on it. And I think it's a great idea. I think it's going to turn out really well, and I'm excited to be a part of it, and I'm excited to hear how it turns out. And Merry Christmas."],["Gram","But he was so nice. He was giving us extra towels. He loved the kids, you know. We had—."],["Mike","What a guy."],["Gram","I tell you, I wouldn't give up those days. They were beautiful. And then the one thing I'll tell on the machine—."],["Gramps","Don't say it."],["Gram","I'm not saying it."],["Gramps","Well, when you're ready, I guess Mike is waiting for you to talk."],["Gram","I am ready. Oh, you mean I could sit here and talk? Oh, that's good."],["Mike","Yeah, we're in position."],["Gram","All right, let me just put this over there."],["Gramps","Watch out for this thing, don't— No, no, I'm good. Oh, Michael, that *-*-*. I forgot all about that. I forgot that one. Holy mackerel."],["Mike","Oh yeah, learning some things about your days in the post office."],["Gramps","Imagine? Oh God."],["Gram","Anybody want a Jell-O with Cool Whip?"],["Mike","No, thank you."],["Christine","No, thank you."],["Gramps","I think Mike wants it. He's smiling. I think he wants— you want one, Mike?"],["Mike F.","Oh no, I'm okay. I'm okay."],["Gram","I gotta show them these. I'll do it after or now."],["Gramps","Sit down."],["Gram","Let me see here. Takes me a while to get back and forth, but I get there."],["Mike","You're fine."],["Gram","Okay. Now my mind's gone blank. What am I supposed to do?"],["Mike","Nothing yet."],["Gramps","Well, which story you want to tell first? Hmm?"]]},"1":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","Which one you want to tell first?"],["Gram","When I was a detective?"],["Gramps","Yeah. Oh, good. That one?"],["Gram","Yeah."],["Mike","I don't think I know this one."],["Gramps","No?"],["Mike","At least not from the little hints that people have been mentioning."],["Gram","Oh, do tell me when I gotta—."],["Mike","Go ahead. You're good. The mic is yours."],["Gram","I've gotta start talking. Okay. My sister lived upstairs in Hoboken and I lived downstairs. And Aunt Betty, it was a coincidence, her husband was working nights and she started getting these obscene phone calls."],["Christine","You don't know this story?"],["Mike","Not yet."],["Gram","And every time she'd get the call after she got done knowing my sister, she'd come running downstairs, Marietta, Marietta, they called again, they called again! I'd say, when they call, just hang up. And this went on. Finally, I got to the point I couldn't stand it anymore. I said, damn it, make a date and I'll meet him. She said, you will. I said, I'll meet him. Make a date with him. So she finally— he called, she made a date. And I worked for Dr. Peluso, which was opposite the park in Hoboken."],["Mike","That was the dentist?"],["Gram","We had it all planned. I told him, Betty, you tell him that I'll meet him at a certain spot. And it happened my boss, Dr. Peluso's office was right there too, overlooking the park. So Grandpa and Dr. Peluso were behind the window. She makes a date, and she says to him, I'll meet you at a certain spot, and I'll wear a red scarf so you'll know it's me. So I said, fine, give me the red scarf. When did you make the date for? We will— I don't have a red scarf. I said, great, now I gotta go out looking for a red scarf. So I find a red scarf. So now we go to the police and tell them the whole story, because I'm not going to meet this guy. And the cops said, good, we'll be in front of the church, by the church, Our Lady of Grace, because that's right opposite the park. And I read in Good Housekeeping, and this was very important, if anybody tries to attack you. have a long nail file in always in your bag, and you get him right here."],["Mike","Jesus!"],["Gram","So I emptied."],["Gramps","Explain what right here. Right here."],["Mike","Let the record show she's pointing and stabbing him in the throat."],["Gram","Gut."],["Mike F.","Stab him with the nail file."],["Gram","Stick it down by his throat."],["Mike","Fine advice."],["Gram","But anyway, I got my bag. I emptied my bag. I put this Mrs. Paluso, the doctor's wife, had a nail file this long. Of course she did. I have it upstairs. I could show it to you. It was. Very long. Put that in my bag. So now I'm all set. I come out of my house, and we still had the grocery store at the time, and my father's standing outside. Where you go, Mariette? I said, oh, I gotta go to Schmidt's Drugstore, which was right where I was really going to be, in that area. I walk with you. I said, no, Papa, I'm a big girl now. You don't have to walk with me, because it was dark. So finally I got rid of my father. Now I'm walking down the street and all of a sudden I see somebody coming up on the other side and he's looking and looking and starts to cross over. He saw the red scarf. So now I'm gonna try and act like a teenager. And I got a son, I mean, how, you know, but Anyway, I forget what his name was. I can't even remember. I don't remember. So he introduces himself. Aunt Betty told him on the phone, my mother and father are going out, so we can go to my house when they leave. So he says, oh, you know, we're going to go to your house. I said, oh, no. I said, they didn't go yet, but they'll be going soon. Let's Walk through the park for a while."],["Gramps","And how old was he?"],["Gram","Oh, he was about 17, 18 years old. That's what I figured anyway, and he was. Now, we start to walk through the park. Now, this side, to the left of the park, there used to be a pencil factory. And they had just knocked the whole factory down, and it was all full of concrete blocks. So I want to walk through the park because That's how they're supposed to. He says, let's walk that way. So I thought, now you're not gonna get me in there. No way. I said, no, I'm gonna walk this way. Now I'm praying to God I don't see the detectives. I can't see anybody. So we're starting to walk and he puts his arm around me. I wanted to punch him right in the nose."],["Mike","What a creep."],["Gram","But anyway, and I'm trying to talk like a teenager. So we start walking, and we're walking a little bit, and of course I'm getting very nervous because I don't— I can't see any of the detectives, and I can't be obvious. But somehow we walked a little while, and I don't know if maybe I turned to look and he got suspicious, so he wanted to—."],["Gram","Start to run. So I saw him lunge. I threw out my foot and knocked him down. And then I threw myself on top of him because I didn't want him to get away."],["Mike F.","You slammed him."],["Gram","And I thought, sweet Jesus, where's the cops? But sure enough, sure enough, they came right away. And I think he came out too with a doctor. I don't remember. But they got him. They put him in a police car. They put me in a police car. We all went to police headquarters. His uncle turned out to be a priest. His mother turned out to be somebody that went to school with Aunt Betty."],["Mike","Mm-hmm."],["Gram","So I don't know the story. But they put me in a room, and they put him in the room while the cops were interviewing his family or whatever. Well, I hate to tell you, I can't say it because I'm on the air, what I said to him. Believe me, I wanted to choke him. Finally, I called the cops and I said, get him out of here. I said, because I'm gonna hit him. I'm gonna hit him. I said, get him out of here."],["Mike","They had just the two of you together?"],["Gram","Just the two of us in the room."],["Mike","That doesn't seem like the way to do it."],["Gram","So I said to the cops, you gotta get him out of here. So that was the story. I had my nail file in my coat pocket."],["Mike","Glad it didn't come to that."],["Gram","But it ended up they put him Well, they wanted to— I don't know if we wanted to press charges, but your uncle was too good. He says he's a young fella, blah blah blah. But they put him in therapy and we talked to his uncle the priest, and that was the end of that. So that was my one experience. I didn't have to use communion."],["Mike","One-day confession, it was all forgiven."],["Gram","Okay."],["Gramps","Now—."],["Mike","That's insane!"]]},"2":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gram","Now what's next?"],["Gramps","Tell them about the—."],["Gram","We went on a trip. My 2 beautiful granddaughters. And we had a new car. So when we were coming out of this— I don't know if it was a store or restaurant— somebody was parking. We were parked. Parking in front of us."],["Gramps","Backing- they were backing into it."],["Gram","But what I saw was where he parked and got out of his car, but I saw our car move a little bit, so I knew he had hit us. Not hard, maybe, but I didn't know. So, and then he took off. So we walked to the car. I looked at our car. There was no damage, but I was so mad that he hit us, I took his front license plate and just bent it in half. And they were shocked. I must have been awfully strong in those days."],["Mike","You were the Hulk when you were younger."],["Gram","I bent it right in half, right in half. So that was that incident. Now let's see what else."],["Mike","I love it. Even as we were thinking on our side, just a couple stories we wanted to tell, they're all just violent or just gumption. It's you standing up to men."]]},"3":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gram","One day we were walking— no, we weren't walking, we were driving down the streets of Hoboken, and, uh, there's a bunch of kids playing ball in the street and blah blah blah. Now these kids, he's tooting the horn Get out of the way. They're not gonna move. I said to him, start moving, slow. Just keep moving. They'll get out of your way."],["Mike","They'll figure it out."],["Gram","They start moving, but as we got like almost in the middle of them, I flung open my door and knocked one guy flat on his ass. And of course—."],["Gramps","And I took right off."],["Mike","Was it a little kid?"],["Gram","My God, we're going to get arrested. And we took off. But they got out of the street once they knew."],["Mike","They learned."],["Gram","That was that. Then let's see."],["Mike","Minimal brain damage."]]},"4":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","Tell them about when you were working for the dentist."],["Gram","I worked as a dental assistant for quite a few years. And I loved— I really loved dentistry. But anyway, every Saturday we used to have an anesthetist come in if you wanted to be knocked out to have teeth pulled."],["Mike F.","Whoa."],["Gram","And he used to come in. So, and when you're under anesthesia, you'd be surprised. You might say things you don't really want people to hear, but you act abnormal. So, okay, the patient is in the chair, the anesthetist is behind him, I'm standing to the one side of the patient, doctor's on the other side. We got— the guy is knocked out and he's getting violent while he's under the anesthesia, and he's shaking and shaking and kicking and kicking, and he had 3 molars that had to come out."],["Gram","So. I was working for him quite a while by then. The doctor said to me, here's the pliers, pull those teeth. And I got nervous because I thought the anesthetist, I mean, you know, you could get arrested for doing it. And I looked and Dr. Putz turned around and said, I'll use my language, goddammit, get those teeth out, Marietta! I got in there with my [inaudible] and I pulled 3 teeth. Didn't break the roots or nothing. Oh! I was good, and that was that story."],["Mike","Had you ever done that before? Pulled teeth?"],["Gramps","Well, how'd you practice?"],["Gram","I used to practice. The doctor, when I first went there-"],["Mike","How does one practice pulling teeth?"],["Gram","-had a lot of skulls."],["Gramps","Yeah, what else?"],["Gram","I used to drill the skulls. I used to make cavities in them and fill them."],["Gramps","What else did you practice on?"],["Mike F.","Did you just say he collected skulls?"],["Gramps","The capuzzelle. The capuzzelle. The goat's head."],["Gram","I can't hear you."],["Gramps","The CAPUZZELLE."],["Gram","Oh yeah, then noonpop and the old Italian men. Used to buy lamb's head. They used to eat them."],["Mike","Oh yeah."],["Gram","When my father was done eating it, I'd get the head and I'd start pulling the teeth. And I used to practice, but"],["Mike","It's gone from cute to concerning."],["Gram","I got those teeth out. That was that. What else?"]]},"5":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Mike","The guy coming in through the back door of the dentist. That's one of my favorites."],["Gramps","Oh, Tony Provenzano."],["Christine","Of course."],["Gram","Oh. Got that one."],["Gram","I'm working and we had a back door and the doctor's family would use it and stuff. And this one day there's a knock at the back door, so I come out and there's 3 men standing there. And they said, we want to come in. I said, you can't come in this way, you've got to go in the waiting room. And he—."],["Gramps","Tell them why they were there. They were part of the The teamsters' tent."],["Gram","Well, I didn't know that at the time."],["Mike","Getting to it."],["Gram","But anyway, they pushed me out of the way. We're coming in! And I said, you can't come in! The doctor heard me hollering. He came out. He said, Marietta, that's okay. Let them in. And we had a little alcove. He said, let them sit there. Then he took me in the back and he said, Marietta, and he told me who they were. They were with the Mafia and all this stuff, and the guy had guns on them. So the doctor was ready for them. He said, come on, assist me. I said, I won't assist you. I won't assist anybody that's got a gun on them. But that was my story with them."],["Gramps","Tony Provenzano."],["Gram","He was connected with the Teamsters."],["Mike","I'm sure."],["Gramps","It was Tony Provenzano."],["Gram","His name was Tony Provenzano. The hood."]]},"6":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gram","One day when we were living in Manalapan, on our way home from work, I stopped at the A&P. We needed milk. And there was this couple walking, too, behind me somewhere. So I got in line and I was going to pay. All of a sudden, I hear this voice, [accent] Well, what do you know? She got ahead of me. What do you think? How quick she snuck in. So I turned around and spoke just like her. [accent] Well, if I got ahead of you and if you were supposed to be first, why the hell ain't you here? And I would be there. So shut up. And this guy's dying. He's dying."],["Christine","Shut up."],["Gramps","That's because it was a Jewish community."],["Gram","It was a very—"],["Christine","We got that."],["Gram","But I had a habit. If somebody spoke to me with a dialect, I don't know why, I would just answer them with their dialect. But this one I wanted it. Okay, that was that."]]},"7":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","Panic, the airplane."],["Gram","The airplane."],["Mike","Which time?"],["Gram","I was trying not to smoke. So we were gonna get on the airplane and we went in the no-smoking section. So I'm by the window. Grandpa's sitting next to me. In front of us is Patty Tosillo and Nancy. So I'm sitting there and I'm scared to death of airplanes. I look down and there's smoke rolling along the bottom. I see the smoke, I tap Patty. Patty, you see smoke? He looks down. Holy God, yeah, there is smoke. Stewardess! There's smoke on the plane! Well I panicked the whole plane. The story was a guy that smoked, and he lit a cigarette, but he wasn't supposed to be smoking, and was holding it down. That's why all the smoke was coming that way. So I panicked the plane. And the stewardess wasn't very happy with me, but too bad. And then another time, Gert and Janie, they were on the plane, and Gert's sitting right next to me, and she goes, puts her hand on her mouth and sort of gasps, and I, I let out a scream. What's happening? What's up? She was yawning. I didn't know that. So again, the stewardess wasn't very happy with me. I don't know anything."],["Gramps","The bus."],["Gram","Huh?"],["Gramps","The bus."],["Gram","Oh, the bus. The post office ran a picnic, a post office picnic, and they hired buses to take us back and forth. So we had a wonderful day, and we all got back on the bus, and I saw the bus driver. He got on, he started the car, the bus, but then I saw him get off. So he left it idling. But I didn't see him, and we were parked on a steep hill. I didn't see him get back on the bus. So all of a sudden, the bus started to move, and I jumped out of my seat screaming, we're going to get killed! The bus is moving! And the bus was fine because the driver was driving. And of course, he wanted to kill me. So I don't know. I seem to get in trouble all the time."]]},"8":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","When you were going to take Stevens to court?"],["Gram","Oh, that's long since—"],["Christine","You're all ready with your prompts."],["Mike","Prompted."],["Gramps","Oh, she's got it all written here."],["Mike","Is this Mr. F? I have a note to ask about Mr. F."],["Gramps","I worked—."],["Mike","I have a note to ask about Mr. F. Is that this one?"],["Gram","Which?"],["Mike","Go on, go on."],["Gram","What?"],["Mike","No, go ahead."],["Gram","When I worked after Paluso, I went to work for Stevens Institute. Which is a big college. And I had a boss, for some reason, just didn't like me. And I really hated him anyway. But he didn't— I don't know why he didn't like me. But anyway, I was there quite a few years. And then this one time we were getting— everybody got a raise. And at that time it was 3 cents an hour. Big deal, but it was a raise. Everybody but Marietta. Didn't get a raise."],["Mike F.","That's nonsense."],["Gram","And I said, no, something's wrong here. I saw the president, I saw everybody, but this guy, Mr. Frisco— he's dead now, it's a good thing— but anyway, he had an in with all the big shots in Stevens. But everybody said, if he doesn't okay your raise, Marietta, we can't. Anyway, okay. So one day, the head of personnel said, I'm going to have a meeting with Mr. Frisco. He was really rotten, this guy. I'm not kidding you. So I said, no, you're not having a meeting with him without me. I'm going to be there, too. So now Mr. Nast has the meeting. I'm sitting down. Mr. Frisco is sitting to my right. And Mr. Nast, the head of personnel, sitting opposite me. Everybody was afraid of this Mr. Frisco. Nobody wanted to cross him. So we're talking, and I worked in Stevens. I started as a part-timer, but I ran the book rush, I ran the campus store. Anytime managers were off, I was thrown into that job. So I really worked mostly full-time. So that's part of the story. So Mr. Frisco turns around and says, oh, I was going to retire. That's what it was. And I said to my— the head of personnel, how much am I going to get as a pension?"],["Gramps","No, wait, wait, wait. Talk about the raise."],["Gram","That's part of the story."],["Gramps","No, it's not the point."],["Gram","What?"],["Gramps","Talk about the raise."],["Gram","Oh, I was on the raise. I'm getting mixed up. So anyway—."],["Mike F.","You went into his office with a— and you were gonna have a meeting with him."],["Gram","What is it Mike?"],["Mike F.","You're gonna have a meeting with him."],["Gram","Yeah, I was at the meeting."],["Mike F.","And everyone was afraid to talk to him, and you were gonna bring up your raise, right?"],["Gram","I can't hear."],["Gramps","Yeah, you were gonna— you brought up the raise, why you were discriminated."],["Gram","Yeah, the raise."],["Gram","They said— I said I was being discriminated against, and they said Mr. Frisco said to the head of personnel, no part-timers get a raise. And I said, I'm more of a full-timer than a part-timer."],["Mike F.","Yeah."],["Gram","So he said something, and I turned around and said, Mr. Frisco, you're a liar. Nobody ever said that to him, honest to God. He got white And he actually lunged, and I think if Mr. Nast wasn't there, he would have slapped me. And the 2 girls that worked in that office were hiding behind the door listening. They were hysterical when I said that. But anyway, came out of there, no raise. Okay. Go downstairs, I called up Mr. Nast and I said, Mr. Nast, I'm not gonna do anything behind anybody's back. I'm taking Stevens to court."],["Mike F.","Yeah."],["Gram","He said—."],["Mike","There you go."],["Gram","Why? What did we do? I said, you're discriminating against me as a person, as a woman. I'm taking you to court."],["Mike F.","Yeah."],["Gram","Okay, 5 minutes later, I got a phone call. You got your raise. I called him back. I wanted retroactive. I'm talking about 3 cents. Yeah, but that was a lot of money."],["Mike F.","That's the principal. Yeah."],["Gram","You got it. Okay, I got everything."],["Christine","Very nice."],["Mike","That's amazing."],["Gram","I got everything."],["Gramps","What's the next thing?"],["Gram","Now the next thing, I'm going to retire."],["Mike F.","Okay."],["Gram","So I meet Mr. Nast, we're waiting to— I was waiting for Sam to pick me up and Mr. Nast was coming down, he was leaving. I said, by the way, he said, I hear you're going to retire, Marietta. I said, yeah, how much pension am I going to get? He said, oh, let's not go through this. I said, I'm telling you now, I'm not leaving unless I get a pension. I told you I wasn't a part-timer. Marietta, we'll talk about it. We sure did talk about it. Y"],["Mike F.","See now he's afraid to negotiate with you."],["Gram","Nobody got a pension. Matter of fact, I ended up I got— I said, I don't care, but I don't care what I get. I get $10.38 a month. That's my pension."],["Gramps","So since 1986, it's never changed. It's $10.38 a month."],["Gram","But I've been getting it for 36 years."],["Mike F.","Oh you still get it?"],["Gramps","Yeah."],["Gram","One of the girls—."],["Mike","That's amazing."],["Gram","She lives in California now, but we're very close. We keep in touch. She was a secretary in personnel, but she was a part-timer. Only about a year ago did she find out— I mentioned something to her— that I get 10. She says, you get a pension? I don't get nothing! I said, you never opened your mouth."],["Mike","You gotta fight for yourself, yeah."],["Gram","You never opened your mouth. Sure enough, I got it. And when I got my first check, my mother happened to be with me, and I said, look, Mom, my first pension. She said, oh, that's wonderful, $1,038 a month. I said, Ma, look at the decimal point. But that was that. But I don't know. I like to think—."],["Gramps","What else? Let me see what else we got on your list."],["Mike","Do you remember the time— you might not— I remember when I was little, you telling the story. I think you might have been at a video store and there was a big, like, a biker in front of you who had long hair. And you, you said you called him ma'am and he turned around. Do you remember that?"],["Gram","I sort of, but now you remember that better than me. Yeah, but I, I just can't help myself."],["Mike","Yeah, he turned around and said something. He said, if you cut your hair, I wouldn't call you ma'am."],["Gram","Probably."],["Christine","That sounds about right."]]},"9":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","Probably. Now, you want to tell a story about when your mother, the bed? Your mother with the bed?"],["Gram","Oh, that's your story. You gotta tell that story."],["Gramps","Alright. Ok. Let me see."],["Mike","Let me see if I got some other notes too."],["Gramps","Hey, Mike, you need a stand-up comedian?"],["Gram","You want me to tell it?"],["Mike F.","I think we're doing a pretty good job already."],["Gram","Well, you want me to tell that story?"],["Gramps","You can tell the story."],["Gram","I mean, it's up to you. We had my mother, she had dementia, and we took care of her. We had made a bedroom You remember Michael, in the living room. And it was funny, she couldn't recognize anybody, but every time he came, she used to call him— he had dancing eyes. She always said that. So anyway, she, she was bedridden, and we had a commode next to the bed, so if she had to use it, we'd get her, put her on it."],["Mike F.","Yeah."],["Gram","So this one night, It was about 9 o'clock at night. We got her off the bed. Grandpa and I got her onto the commode. She did what she had to do. Now, we're going to put her back in bed. Well, somehow she's— he's trying to get her in bed, she slips, and he threw himself under her. So that she wouldn't hit the floor. So Mama ended up sitting on Grandpa's lap on the floor. Now I'm looking at the two of them. I can't pick them up. So I said, what are we going to do now? So I called the emergency squad. These two guys come in and the first thing they do, they open the door and they look and they see—."],["Mike F.","They're having a good time."],["Gram","My mother's sitting on his lap on the floor. And they just cracked up. It was a sight to see. They cracked up. They had to get— first they had to get my mother into bed and then help him up. But that was the funniest. If anything could be funnier than that, I can't believe it."],["Gramps","That's your story."],["Gram","That's that story."]]},"10":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","Oh, no, one more."],["Gram","What?"],["Gramps","Your boss Joe Introne."],["Gram","That I held him up against the machine?"],["Mike","Yeah. There's definitely a theme through all these stories."],["Gram","I tell you, we had my boss in the print shop, Frisco, this guy, he was like the head of the department. But then I had a boss that came in, and his name was Joe Introne. He was a jerk, honest to God. And he resented me because I knew more than him. See, they offered me the job as manager, but I didn't want it."],["Mike F.","Why?"],["Gram","So then they hired this guy. Anybody who walked in the print shop, my desk was the first thing you'd meet. [phone rings in background] Whoever it is, you call him. Don't even answer. Oh, go ahead. Call him back. Can you keep talking? Stop?"],["Christine","Go ahead."],["Gramps","[in background] Hello?"],["Gram","I can talk. So anyway, he just didn't— like the President's office would give us big jobs, and they'd explain it to me and then I'd explain it to him. He resented that. He didn't want them to talk to me."],["Mike","Yeah."],["Gram","So this woman that worked for the President came down one day and I told her, I said, you can't tell me no more, Charlotte. You got to tell Joe. Is he still on the phone? So she went in and she was very She was nice. She said, Joe, what do I hear about this, I can't tell Marietta? She said, well, I'm telling you now. We have a job, we explain it to her, and then she takes over to you. So he resented me, he really did. One day, Gramps had gone there, he had stopped there, or he needed copies in the post office, and we'd always help him out. But after he left, he was very nice to Grandpa, but when he left, I heard him knocking him to one of the pressmen."],["Mike F.","Yeah."],["Gram","And Joe was about— he was a little taller than me and heavy. And we had negative machines where we made plates and stuff. So I went over to him and I says, I heard you talking, Joe. I grabbed him like this-"],["Mike F.","Right around the collar"],["Gram","-and I pushed him right into the machine. And I said, one more word out of you, and brother, you're on that floor. And he was very nice after that."],["Mike F.","Was he shaking too?"],["Gram","He knew I meant business. That was Joe Introne. And that— I think that's— and that— this is my life. I get in trouble no matter what I do."],["Christine","We know where Linda gets it from."],["Mike F.","You're sticking it to the man."],["Gramps","That's it."],["Gram","Oh God."],["Gramps","You got no more. That's it, you're finished."],["Mike","Wow."],["Gram","I think I'm finished."],["Gramps","I got nothing to say."],["Mike","Geez, you're gonna—."],["Gramps","I've got nothing to say. Not after that. Come on, I'm not gonna follow that act. That's your grandma."],["Mike","Geez. You're gonna come off like a sweetheart after this. Geeze."],["Gram","That's probably more than you guys remember about me, but I-"],["Mike","I'm glad I was on your good side."],["Gram","-just don't want anybody make a fool of me. That's it."],["Mike","Fair enough."],["Christine","That's very fair."],["Gram","You don't fight for—."],["Gramps","You never heard some of those stories?"],["Mike","A couple of them I never heard."],["Gram","Yeah."],["Mike","Geez. I guess you kept it from me."],["Gram","I remember their expression when I bent that. Gram! Oh God."]]},"11":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gram","But anyway, this is quite an experience. Quite an experience. Janie said I used to have an Australian accent."],["Gramps","I don't know where that came from."],["Gram","But every time I used to go to the bakery by your mother, Remember, we used to buy cake."],["Gramps","They weren't Australian. They were German. They were German."],["Gram","Then I had a German accent in there."],["Gramps","You used to bring some kind of a cake-"],["Gram","Boston Cream Pie."],["Gramps","-when you used to go see the girls."],["Gram","Huh?"],["Gramps","When you used to go see Jackie when the girls were born, you used to stop there and you used to bring cake there. A certain cake you used to bring there."],["Gram","Where?"],["Gramps","In Glen Rock."],["Gram","Oh, when they were—."],["Gramps","Yeah, that's the German. It was German. They were German."],["Gram","They were German. Harold's. Uh, Schoening's."],["Gramps","No, you used to go—."],["Gram","No, I used to— well, I don't know."],["Gramps","On Bergenline Avenue, you used to stop and get it."],["Gram","That's when I used to go to your mother's. Jackie, I never went on Bergenline Avenue."],["Gramps","If you don't stop, I'm taking you to court."],["Gram","You better stop. That's why we're married 71 years. He knows better."],["Mike F.","That's a long time."],["Mike","Yeah, he knows better."],["Gram","He knows better."],["Mike","He's seen you in action. He doesn't want to mess with you."],["Gramps","Is it my turn now?"],["Mike","Yeah, do you have a list for yourself?"],["Gramps","I don't know what the heck to say. I got nothing."],["Gram","I remember Uncle Tommy used to come down the stairs when he heard me coming home, how'd you do today, Marietta? How'd you do? And he'd say, why don't you make Sammy talk? I'll take care."]]},"12":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gram","Oh, when I threw the politicians out of our house."],["Gramps","Oh, that's another story."],["Gram","Turn it on. He's got enough."],["Christine","Oh, keep going."],["Gram","No, no. I was president of a political association."],["Gramps","Which I objected to."],["Gram","Yes, he objected."],["Mike","For the record."],["Gramps","No, you know why? Because I was in the post office."],["Mike","It was a conflict of interest."],["Gramps","Yeah, I didn't want it, I said-"],["Mike","I see that"],["Gram","This one year, his name was Thomas A. Gallo. He was running, and this Fred DiSapio was running against him. Of course, I worked for Gallo. He got elected, and that was good. A couple of years later, there was a new election, and this Fred DiSapio was running, and Gallo turned against him. I just didn't like it, so I decided—."],["Gramps","You left."],["Gram","So I decided. I wasn't president anymore. I'm going to work with DiSapio. So I'm working for him. Not that I'm working, I'm, you know, what you do during an election, you talk up. So this one night, he was working nights, Conrad was little, he was sound asleep, my bell rang. And it's Gallo with one of his cronies, Frank."],["Gramps","Yeah."],["Gram","Which I hated him."],["Gram","I said, why are you coming up here? He said, want to talk to you. And they're giving me this thing about, how could you do this? You're working for him. You got to work for me. I let them talk. I says, okay. Now get up and get the hell out of my house. Don't you dare come in my house uninvited. I says, you, you loved this guy. Now I said, I hate politicians. You love each other, then you hate each other. I said, I'm doing what I want. Don't ever come in my house uninvited. And they lived on our block previously, so I knew them well."],["Mike","Yeah."],["Gram","I went to his wife the next day. She lived on Hudson Street, they lived. And I told her what I did. I said, Addie, I want you to know why I threw your husband out of my house. She said, you did the right thing."],["Mike","I would have done it too."],["Gram","And that was the end of that. And DeSapio won. DeSapio won."],["Mike","There you go."],["Gram","Oh, I had a guy ring my bell not long ago, and he was running for something up here. He's talking up a storm. I said, look, you know what I got to say about politicians? I haven't met an honest one yet. I said so."],["Mike","Get the hell out of my house, sir."],["Gram","He said to me, you didn't say that. I understand. I said, you heard me. I haven't, I haven't met an honest politician yet, and that's the truth. But this has been quite an experience in my life. Now, this is your life, Marietta. Boy, I was rough."]]},"13":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","You got any tape left on there?"],["Mike F.","What's that?"],["Gramps","You got any tape left?"],["Christine","Yeah, it's your turn."],["Mike F.","It never ends."],["Gramps","I don't think you're gonna find this as good as hers."],["Gram","Oh, what do you got?"],["Mike","I find that hard to believe."],["Gramps","Geez. It's blank. Yeah, uh, let me see what I'm gonna say. Let's see, this is December 26th, 2018, so I guess this is a version of the old TV show This Is Your Life many years ago. So I'll try. I don't think I'm going to beat the gal that was ahead of me, but anyway. She's a tough act to follow. You know that stuff, Mike, right? Tough acts to follow."],["Mike F.","Yeah she is."],["Gramps","But anyway, I was born in— my name is Sam Tridente, okay? I was born May 20th, 1925. When I was born, growing up, I was called Sam or Sammy. But actually, I found out later that wasn't my name. My name was Sergio. And how the name Sergio is, when you're married and you have a boy, the firstborn is named after your father's father, which would have been my grandfather. His name was Sergio."],["Gramps","Okay? If we had a girl, the firstborn would be named after the mother's side."],["Gram","So what's so exciting?"],["Mike","We got a heckler."],["Gramps","So anyway, now growing up, when I was getting confirmed, I was going to make confirmation, you get a sponsor. To confirm you, and you take a middle name. I took my uncle as my sponsor to be my godparent at confirmation. His name was Mario, but we called him Morris. Okay? So when I filled out my papers, I put Sam Morris Tridente, and I went through the classes, and I didn't think nothing of it. When they made— when we got confirmed and they made the announcement from the pulpit and the bulletin they put out, My name wasn't Sam Morris Tridente; it was Samuel Maurice Tridente. So from the minute I saw that, I said, \"I will never use my middle name.\" I guess the nuns didn't like Sam and they didn't like Morris, so they made it Samuel Maurice. So I've never used my middle name."]]},"14":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","Okay, next. I gotta think. My life wasn't that exciting. No, not not like my wife's. Uh, let's see. When I joined the Navy— and the reason I joined the Navy wasn't because I wanted to be a hero or see action and all that stuff. I was 17 years old when the war started. I was 16 years old when the war started. And when the draft came out, men had to register for the draft at the age of 21. So I figured I was only 16, I was safe, I didn't have to worry about it."],["Gramps","Because if I had to go, I didn't want to go into the Army or the Marines. I wanted to figure the Navy. And the reason I wanted to go into the Navy, because at 16, all I could picture was a big German guy coming at me with a bayonet, or going into the Pacific and a little Japanese guy getting at me. So I figured the Navy, they're over there and I'm over here, I'm safe. But anyway, so I figured I was safe. I figured the war would be over by the time I'm 21. And in 1942, the president changed the rules. He said draft age will be 18. Now I'm closer to 18, okay?"],["Mike F.","Yeah."],["Gramps","And I'm saying to myself, oh my God, I've got to do something about it. In November of '42, I decided to go over to New York to enlist."],["Mike F.","OK."],["Gramps","Not telling my parents. In the Navy. I went over. I went over. I get an exam. I passed a couple of the parts of it. Doctor got to my ear and he says, you got a perforated left eardrum. He says, you're unfit for duty. Oh, I says, yeah? Any branch of the service? He says, no, you'd be unfit for any branch of the service. Okay. I went home. I told my parents. They weren't too happy that I had tried to enlist, but they were happy that I got rejected."],["Mike F.","Yeah."],["Gramps","Now, as time went on and I got closer to my 18th birthday, I'm saying to myself, God forbid I go for an examination because I have to register. And for some reason I pass and they put me in the Marines or the Army, I'll be sick. So I decided to go back again. So I went back again without telling my parents, went through the examination again. The guy got to my ear, didn't say nothing, and passed me right along. All of a sudden I had a—."],["Mike","They were more desperate."],["Gramps","You're right. They were desperate. So I passed. But he says, you gotta have your papers, your parents sign your papers because you're 17. You have to have your parents' permission. I went home, told my parents. Again, they weren't happy, but I said, Mom, Dad, I don't want to go into the Army or the Marines. So they consented and I went into the Navy. That's that part. That's the part. And I didn't go in because I wanted to be a hero."],["Mike","The exact opposite."],["Gramps","I just didn't want to go in the Army or the Marines, okay?"],["Gram","You didn't have a very exciting life."],["Mike","Geez. I think your life is great."]]},"15":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","No, what else?"],["Mike","What about the first time you went out and they sent the message?"],["Gram","Oh yeah."],["Gramps","Oh. Now, when I was in the Navy, after boot camp, they assign you to what trade you're gonna be, and they put me to be a signalman, okay? So I went to signal school. I was a pretty good student. Out of a class of 160, they promoted— usually you went in as a seaman second class. If you passed the course after 8 weeks, you became seaman first class. But if you had exceptional marks, they promoted 10% of the class. We were 160, so out of 160, 16 guys were promoted."],["Gramps","And I got promoted. Ooh, nice job. Which I wasn't even thinking about it. But anyway, so I got stripes. Now, I'm 18 years old and I got stripes, which is equivalent to a sergeant in the Army. Big deal, okay? Now I get assigned to a ship. My first trip out. Now in school, when you're studying blinker lights, the lights, the signals, it's in the same place all the time. It's not moving."],["Mike F.","Okay."],["Gramps","Okay? Now on a ship, when you're leaving port, and we were leaving San Francisco, up on the hilltop was a signal station and they blinked my call letters of the ship and I flashed back to say, okay, continue with the message. The ship is moving now, okay? The light is blinking and after every word when you receive it, you blink back to let them know you got it so they continue. That light kept blinking. I never blinked back. The light kept blinking. I never blinked back. The captain's alongside of me, my commanding officer's alongside of me, and the ship is moving. We get underneath the Golden Gate Bridge and out we go. And I never got the message. I wanted to die. I wanted to die. But they were very nice. They knew I was a greenie. But I mean, here I am, I'm a petty officer and I didn't know what the hell was going on."],["Mike","I've always been convinced you would have The war would have ended if we had just gotten that message."],["Christine","Right?"],["Gramps","And you know what the message was?"],["Mike","You found out?"],["Gramps","Oh yeah. Of course after that I knew. After you passed under the Golden Gate Bridge, the message was you can have target practice. But the weather is always garbagey after the Golden Gate Bridge, so there was never any gunnery practice. But that was the message. But I wanted to— that was an embarrassing moment."],["Mike","I didn't know you ever found out what it was."],["Gramps","No. It was the same message every time you left San Francisco it was the same message. So I knew that was it."]]},"16":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","Okay. Now, as a kid, I had an uncle. I guess I was the only nephew in the family at the time. Any occasion, he used to always give me something, my birthday or anything. He gave me my first pair of roller skates."],["Gram","Uncle Moe?"],["Gramps","No, Uncle Johnny."],["Gram","Oh, Uncle Johnny."],["Gramps","He gave me my first pair of roller skates, my first ice skates, my first bicycle, ahh oh, when portable radios came out, it was a Zenith. It had a battery about this long, heavy as anything. And he gave me that. Okay?"],["Mike","Uncle Johnny."],["Gramps","Yeah. Yeah. What the heck was I— oh, I'm trying to remember why I was talking about that."],["Gram","Uncle Johnny giving you gifts."],["Gramps","Yeah, but what?"],["Mike F.","After the Navy, Uncle Johnny, first bikes."],["Mike","Back when he was a kid."],["Gramps","Oh, okay. So when I was in high school and he had given me my first bike, I got a job in the summertime working for a vegetable store, okay, on the main street in Hoboken. And right around the corner was the Catholic church with the nuns. And they were customers. Now, this guy that owned the vegetable store, I used to deliver vegetables after people ordered, okay?"],["Mike","Mm-hmm."],["Gramps","And the nuns always ordered something and I used to deliver them, so I got to know them. This guy used to like to gamble. I guess he used to go make his bets for the horses. And he used to leave me alone in the store, okay? Now, I was a high school kid. One day, I'm in there alone. And a guy comes in and says, I'm from St. Peter and Paul's Church. The nuns sent me here to order stuff. Oh, I said, okay. So he orders about $5 or $6 worth of stuff. I pack it up. Oh, he says, you know what, he says, you can deliver it. I said, well, it's $5 and some— oh, he says, yeah, by the way, he says, the nuns told me to give me the change of $20, the difference, because I have to make another stop for them. And when you deliver the vegetables to them, they'll give you the $20."],["Mike F.","Oh, that's sneaky."],["Mike","Yeah."],["Gram","Talk a little louder."],["Gramps","So I said, OK. The boss came back. I told him the story. He says, oh, no. I said, what's the matter? He says, Sam, go ahead, deliver the vegetables, he said, but I don't think I don't think they're from there."],["Mike","Yeah."],["Gramps","So I brought the vegetables to the—."],["Gram","Talk louder. Talk louder. I can't hear you too good. I want to hear what happened, too."],["Gramps","She's got to get into the act. Okay. So anyway, I went with my bicycle, took the vegetables there, saw the nuns. I got to know them. And they said, we didn't order anything."],["Mike F.","Oh."],["Gramps","Okay. So I went back to the store. The guy scammed me, okay? Now, we reported to the police station and I said to the boss, I said, geez, I'm sorry. I didn't realize. He said, that's all right. He said, what we'll do, he said, we'll take it out of your pay until you— for the difference. So I had to pay like $15. So I was making maybe $2.50 for the week that I worked there."],["Mike","Geez."],["Gramps","So I worked all that time. When I paid the bill, then I quit. I figured, I figured you're not gonna— I mean, imagine I was a kid."],["Mike","It's not your fault."],["Gramps","Yeah, but I got scammed."]]},"17":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","Then there was another time. This was in the Navy, my first trip. Now we're greenhorns, we're rookies, we've never been out to sea before. And a lot of the Navy crew were new, new guys that hadn't been to sea before. Our first port of call was New Guinea, Milne Bay, New Guinea. We laid— sometimes in some islands there was no docks or piers, so you used to drop anchor out in the stream and then they used to have the amphibious, the ducks."],["Mike","Yep."],["Gramps","Okay? They'd come alongside and they'd unload the cargo one at a time. So we were there 30 days by the time we got unloaded. We're scheduled to leave at 12 o'clock this one day after 30 days we were there. Before we're leaving, a launch comes up and who comes aboard but a sergeant in the Army. He wants to see the commanding officer. So commanding officer gets notified and he makes the announcement, I want all crew members up on the deck."],["Gram","Okay."],["Gramps","So we all go up there. So the sergeant says, fellas, he says, any of you guys that bought grass skirts, bring them up here. Okay."],["Gramps","He says, okay. And he said, I'm going to give you cardboard. Put your name, who you want it sent to. And we said, what's it for? He said, well, he says, you know, you can't take these to the States. They've got to be fumigated. Okay? So all us rookies, we put our name, we put the tag on it, and he says, once they're fumigated, they will be sent to the destination. This is the year 2018. They have never come back. Okay?"],["Gram","Tell them about your mother with the roses."],["Gramps","Oh yeah. So what he had, he had a deal, I guess, with a native. I think I paid about $3 for the grass skirt. I wasn't going with your mother at that time, your grandmother at the time. But I was going to send them home to my mother, you know. So he must have got them. He knew when the ship was sailing, took them, gave it to his cohort native, and resold them again. So I got—."],["Mike","What a racket. That's a good racket."],["Gramps","Good racket."],["Gram","Tell them about the pictures you— But talk loud."],["Gramps","I can't hear you. When I was going with your grandmother, we had made a trip to Honolulu. Now, in Honolulu, They had, on the streets, they had different sections and girls in grass skirts. They weren't naked, they just had grass skirts and a bra and all that. And they had their stands set up taking your picture. You know, it was like 50 cents a picture every time you took a picture with them. So I took a couple of pictures with them and I sent them home to your grandmother. When she got them, she showed my mother. And my mother, when she saw them, she ripped them right up. She says, what is my son doing sending you pictures with somebody else?"],["Gram","I said, what are you doing? She said, he's going with you. He shouldn't be doing this."],["Gramps","So I have no pictures with—."],["Mike","No grass skirt, no pictures."],["Gramps","I'm telling you, I couldn't believe it. That she did that."]]},"18":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","Trying to think of any other stories."],["Mike","What about when you were pursuing her?"],["Christine","Yes."],["Gramps","Oh, you want to hear that story?"],["Christine","Yes!"],["Mike","Yeah, I do."],["Gramps","Okay. Uh, it was 1942."],["Gram","Talk loud, Sam. I want to hear."],["Gramps","I was— it was 1942. I was just out of high school. I didn't want to go to college. I wanted to be a lawyer growing up, but then as I got close to graduating, I didn't— we're not as smart as the kids today at 17, okay? And all I knew about lawyers when I thought about it is that they lie a lot because they're defending people."],["Mike","Yep."],["Gramps","So I said, that's not for me. So when my father said, you don't want to go to college? I says, no, Papa, I just don't want to go. Okay."],["Gramps","He says— oh no, okay, yeah, okay. So now I'm looking for a job, okay? In fact, I tried for a job in Macy's. Now I'm not qualified for anything, so the only thing I could ask for is to be a stock clerk. Now believe it or not, Macy's in New York City, I went over, they give you some test like you're going to be an architect or something. They evaluate you. And I think the pay was like 45 cents an hour. Okay? So in the meantime, I connected in Hoboken with Chilton's, the makers of Old Spice, and they were paying 45 cents an hour. So I decided to go there. But before I got a job, I used to walk the streets and where Grams lived, it was only a block away from where we lived on 3rd Street."],["Mike","Okay."],["Gramps","So one day I'm walking down the street and I see this nurse walking towards me. As she got closer, I said, she's not bad. She's a good-looking gal."],["Mike F.","Did she have a grass skirt on?"],["Gramps","No, no, no. And believe it or not, this is the truth. I said, this gal is beautiful. And this is the girl I have to meet because I'm gonna marry her. That's the God's honest truth. I was 17. So as she passed me by, I turned around and started following her. Okay? Today they call it stalking, but I was following her."],["Mike","Tale as old as time."],["Gramps","She walked a little bit, turned a corner, and went into a house. So I figured that's where she lived. Okay? So that was it. So every day, well, before I got a job, I used to pass the house just to see if I'd see her. Had no luck. Then one day I passed and I see her in front of a grocery store across the street from where she lived talking to a kid that I knew oh I says."],["Mike","Wearing a red scarf."],["Gramps","So I said, this is great. I said, I'll go up there and he'll introduce me. By the time I got to them, she crossed the street and went into the house, but he was still there. So I say, hey, Petey, how you doing? Oh, good, good, good. I says, who's that girl you were talking to? Oh, he says, her name is Marie. This is what he said. Her name is Marie, and she lives across the street. I says, oh, I says, she's a nice-looking girl. He says, yeah, she's my girlfriend."],["Gram","God bless him."],["Gramps","So all of a sudden, I says, all of a sudden, he ruined my day, OK? So I forgot about her. So I never did see her again. Then I got a job. I didn't have time to be walking the streets anymore. And then I went into the Navy. Never saw her again. But I used to think about her, OK?"],["Gram","You wrote me a letter."],["Gramps","Yeah. I went into the Navy and I wrote her a letter, because she had told me her name and where she lived and where she wasn't a nurse, she worked for the dentist, but they used to wear nurses' uniforms."],["Mike","Sure."],["Gramps","And I wrote her a letter, told her who I was. I only lived a block away from there. But the block away, the guys weren't all that great. I mean, I was a little weird. I was a little different from the rest of them. And we had all kinds of guys. In that group. But anyway, I wrote her, told her who I was. I said, would you like to write to me? She never answered my letter."],["Gram","I took the letter, threw it away. [inaudible] with that rough gang. I don't want to know him."],["Gramps","I came home on leave and I said, geez, maybe I'll walk around, maybe I'll spot her again. And one day, and while, no..."],["Gram","But you started sending me gifts from showtime."],["Gramps","No, that was after we were going together. So I came home on leave and I couldn't see her again. I couldn't really see her again. And then one day I was there and I said, maybe I can see her. And she came out and there was a girl waiting there for her. I said, are you waiting for that Marie in there? She says, yeah. Oh, I says, good, I'd like to see her. So I figured she'd introduce me to her, okay? She comes out, talks to her girlfriend, was going to introduce me to her. Oh, she said, you're the guy that sent me a letter? I says, yeah. I says, you didn't answer me. She says, I don't write to people I don't know, okay? And with that she walks away. Okay? Now I'm left with her girlfriend. I says, you know what, I said, would you write to me? Okay? But only because I figured I'd touch base with her and she'd talk to her."],["Mike","Stay connected."],["Gramps","So I did. She corresponded with me and I corresponded. But I always spoke about her. Now a year goes by, I'm on a ship, I come home again. On leave. I'm going to get assigned to another ship, but I'm home on, I think, a 20-day leave, whatever it was. And again— oh, and in the meantime, I had relatives that lived in Oakland, California. My ship used to pull into San Francisco. So when my ship pulls into San Francisco, I used to visit them. And on one of the occasions, a friend of my uncle, we're talking. Oh, he says, I have relatives in Hoboken. I said, oh, you do? He says, yeah. I said, what's their name? And he says, Cappelluti, and they have a grocery store. I said, holy mackerel. I didn't say nothing until—."],["Mike","That's weird."],["Gramps","Yeah."],["Mike","Fate."],["Gramps","This is a true story. I says, oh, good. So I'm getting all the information from him. I get his name and all that, and I figure I'm coming home on leave. I come home on leave. I figure the first thing I'm doing, I'm going to that store, introduce myself, and say, hey, your cousin in California said to say hello."],["Gram","My father was very strict."],["Mike","Terrifying."],["Gram","We weren't allowed to date. God forbid my father saw us talking to a fella, he'd kill us. So."],["Gramps","So I went in, I met the father. He talked to me. He was very nice. I met the grandfather. He used to help him there. Talked to him. Very nice. And that was it. But the next day I went by her office again."],["Gram","But say, tell him what Grandpa said."],["Gramps","Well, that was later on. He said, I don't want you to—."],["Gram","No, that day."],["Gramps","He didn't say it to me."],["Gram","Not to you. Grandpa was in the store when you told Papa the story."],["Gramps","Yeah."],["Gram","My grandfather came in and I was inside with my mother and all. He said to my mother, I think I just met your new son-in-law."],["Mike F.","Wow."],["Gram","He almost got a beating."],["Christine","Aw."],["Gram","I didn't even know him. That was what my grandfather said."],["Gramps","So the next day I went by the office, figuring it was lunchtime, she's going to come out. She sees me standing there and I says, you know, I got to— she says, oh, you're the guy that was in my father's store yesterday? I says, yeah. She says, why did you do that? I said, because I want to meet you, okay? So we got to talk and she didn't run away this time. I said, you know—"],["Mike","Always a good start."],["Gramps","-Would you like to have lunch with me or dinner with me someday? For some reason she said yes."],["Gram","I thought I could get rid of you."],["Gramps","So I took a day off. It was like a Wednesday."],["Gram","But it was in the afternoon."],["Gramps","Yeah."],["Gram","Because I wasn't allowed to date. And Wednesdays we worked half a day. So I said to myself, hey, I've got a date. I'll get a free lunch. And we met."],["Gramps","So I said, I'll take you to China Clipper. It was up in Jersey City. It was a nice restaurant."],["Gram","China Clipper."],["Gramps","China Clipper. So we sat down. Now, we're ordering and the waitress or the waiter says, what would you like to drink? I said—."],["Gram","How I hate it."],["Gramps","I said, she'll have milk."],["Gram","He orders milk and I hate milk. I mean, he didn't give me a chance to say what I'd like. Oy. I was dying."],["Gram","He said, what I like about you-"],["Mike","Smooth."],["Gram","—is you don't drink and you don't smoke. I was dying for a cigarette and a nice rum and Coke. And he ordered me a glass of milk."],["Gramps","But anyway, from that date, I guess something must have happened to her. We had another date. We went to New York City roller skating, the Gay Blades."],["Gram","On a Wednesday afternoon."],["Gramps","Gay Blades."],["Mike","The Gay Blades?"],["Gramps","That was the name of the— Gay Blades. I got a thing with those ***. So anyway—"],["Mike F.","Did that guy happen to have a mailbox that you [inaudible]?"],["Gramps","No, that was the name of the place, Gay Blades."],["Mike","Alright."],["Gramps","Because I had never been in a roller skating rink before. Our skating was always in the streets. OK?"],["Gram","I didn't even know how to roller skate. Before you, all the sailors used to be there and they used to hold me and we used to— they taught me how to skate."],["Gramps","Yeah, I know they taught you. I know. And you didn't want to talk to me. But anyway, after that, I guess we dated some more. I don't know how— where else we went."],["Gram","When you started sending me—."],["Gramps","No, no, honey. No, no, no."],["Gram","The Viola. From Shulte's."],["Gramps","Wait a minute. Wait a minute. We had— we went a couple more places. I forget where else we went. But then I had to go back to my— by that time we were a couple. Now, in the Italian families, if you're going to become a couple, the parents have to meet. So my parents had to come down to meet her family and we sat down for— I guess we became an item."],["Gram","We had to be for your family to-"],["Gramps","So anyway, we became a couple. I went back to the West Coast, picked up another ship, and it was like a year later before I came back. Again."],["Gram","But before we became a couple, you forgot, used to send me gifts. That's what made me like you more. He worked with a woman by the name of Viola."],["Gramps","Viola, she was a good gal."],["Gram","And he'd have her send me—."],["Gramps","She was a secretary in the office and stuff."],["Gram","-Perfumes and stuff. He grew on me."],["Gramps","She figured, I gotta— I got a guy that's giving me things."],["Mike","Free lunches and gifts. What's not to like?"],["Gram","Because I used to— you know. I wasn't allowed to date. So Wednesday afternoons, I'd be able to tell my father I was going to New York or something by myself. But I had a date with some sailors. A matter of fact, Dr. Peluso, I could go to any theater in New York. He had connections."],["Mike","Apparently."],["Gram","And just mention his name, and they would let me in. So one day I went with 3 sailors."],["Mike","Geez. Stop this story right now."],["Gram","And one sailor said I reminded him of his English teacher, and he was madly in love with me. And I said, forget it. So then one day I get this ring in the mail. It was his high school or college ring. He was dead serious. He wanted to go out with me. Thank God he had his return address on the ship, whatever, because I immediately— and I wrote and told him, I said, stop writing to me because that's it. You know, there's nothing. I don't care what you feel for me. There's nothing. And that was the end of that."],["Gramps","And then I ended up with her."],["Gram","I'm glad."],["Mike","So are we."],["Gram","Even though you ordered me milk. Oh God. When he said—."],["Mike","Why milk? What a choice."],["Gram","-You know what I like about you? You don't drink and you don't smoke. And I thought, Jesus, there's the glass of milk and I want to have a cigarette and a rum and Coke. I took you to New York. I used to take you out."],["Gramps","You know why? You know why? She was making more money than me when I was working. That's why."],["Gram","I made good money."],["Gramps","Can I finish?"],["Mike","She assaulted people until they gave him her raise."],["Gramps","I mean, you had your time on the mic. Can I have my time now?"],["Gram","Yeah, but talk loud so I can hear. I'm enjoying hearing this."]]},"19":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","Now, the other, growing up as a kid, okay, I was born during the Depression time. Depression time was when the economy was no good. There was no jobs available. We didn't feel it. My father worked on the piers. And for some reason, I guess dock workers were working. So we never felt the Depression. A lot of families really hurt. And women, the mothers, the wives, they used to take— they used to call sewing buttons. There was somehow where you sewed buttons on a card and they got paid so much for doing that. My family never felt that. I was never allowed to wear dungarees. Dungarees was a sign of being poor. So I never wore dungarees. Okay? When I went into the Navy and got my dungarees and I came home on leave, I wore them. I said, Pop, I got dungarees. Okay? The other story is growing up, we wore knickers. Knickers was a big thing. My first pair of long pants was was when I went to high school. Now, high school in Hoboken only held 9th, 10th— held 10th, 11th, and 12th grades. It wasn't big enough for the 9th grade. So we had to go to another school for 7th, 8th, and 9th, and they called that junior high."],["Mike","Yup."],["Gramps","So when I graduated in 1939 from junior high, which was actually the first year of high school, but only with I got my first pair of long pants. Man, that's something, my first pair of long pants. But otherwise it was knickers. You know the story about knickers, right? Oh man."],["Gramps","I told Ryan the story. He says, wait a minute, I'm gonna check it out. He sent me a picture of knickers. I said, I don't know what that— he said, it looks like an elephant. I says, no. I said, I don't know where you got those knickers from. But anyway, That was my first pair of long pants. Now, growing up as a kid, again, times were tough. I didn't feel it. Kids from the poor families used to shine shoes. They had shoeboxes. They used to shine shoes or sell newspapers."],["Mike","Yeah."],["Gramps","I wasn't allowed to do that. Okay? But I used to hang out with guys. So I used to help them. They had their own shoe shine box. But they didn't do it in Hoboken. They used to go to New York. Now, to get to New York, you had the ferry boat. And from Hoboken to New York, it was easy to sneak onto the ferry boat. OK? And it was like a nickel. We were able to sneak on. Coming back was a little tougher, but we were able to do it. So I used to go with them and help them shine shoes. OK? I wanted to have my own shoeshine box, okay? So in Hoboken, on First Street, there was a lot of— we used to call them cheap stores. They used to sell all kinds of cheap stuff, shoe polish and all that stuff, and it was cheap. So every time I had a couple of pennies, I used to go- [ringing phone] oh, gosh."],["Gram","Go ahead."],["Gramps","Every time I had a couple of pennies, I used to go buy shoe polish, buy a brush, I got a box. I made up a box. I was all set to do my own shoeshine. My father— I had it down in the cellar, down in the basement. My father finds it and he shows it to me and he says, what are you doing with this? I said, Papa, I thought—. He says, I'm gonna break this and you're not gonna do shining shoes. I said, Pop, wait a minute, wait a minute. Leave it alone. I'll take everything back. I'll get some money back. I didn't want to lose. So I did take it. He said, okay. So I took everything back. So I didn't shine shoes. Selling newspapers, we had a local newspaper company in Hoboken, the Jersey Journal. And if you bought— and the kids used to sell newspapers on the corner."],["Gram","Yeah. They threw the envelopes away. Wait, I'm gonna let you tell Sammy, Mary. He can hear better than me. Mary Ruppel."],["Gramps","Mary?"],["Gram","The church."],["Gramps","What happened?"],["Gram","We gave her 2 envelopes with masses in it, and I think she's saying she—."],["Gramps","Wait, wait, wait, Mary, let me get the other phone. Hold on, Mary, hold on, let me get the other phone. I can't hear her."],["Gram","I think she destroyed them. He has to go to the corded phone. Can't hear good enough."],["Mike","Every time."],["Gramps","Yeah, Mary, what's happening?"],["Gram","You know, we were kept so strict. Like, even when we were keeping company, we were engaged, everything. When Gramps would come to the house every night, you know, to stop, my father would out-sit him. My father wouldn't go to bed until he left. It was really almost ridiculous, you know. Oh God,"],["Christine","I can imagine that."],["Gramps","I don't know what she did. I don't know. Listen, you guys hungry?"],["Mike","No, I just had my sandwich."],["Gram","I made something. I could— I don't know, I could heat it up."],["Mike","I'm good. My sandwich wasn't as big as Gramps, but it was— my sandwich wasn't as big as Gramps, but it was still big."],["Gramps","Uh, this 412 isn't no good."],["Gram","I couldn't understand what you said."],["Gramps","Let me check my checkbook and I'll make up a new one for you, okay, Mary?"],["Gram","I don't know what the heck she did."],["Gramps","Okay, okay."],["Gram","Because I gave her an envelope with Aunt Betty died Christmas Eve, so we always have masses said on Christmas Eve for Aunty Betty."],["Gram","I don't know what she did. And our church has been without heat for over a month."],["Mike","Why?"],["Gram","Yesterday, I thought I was gonna die. It was so cold in church."],["Gramps","Oh, I'm sorry to hear that."],["Gram","I said to the priest, I said, Father, I can't keep coming here."],["Mike","Yeah."],["Gram","You know, to church. So he said, well, they're working on it, Marietta. Hopefully they'll have it. It's a month."],["Mike","That's a long time."],["Gramps","Okay, I'll take care of it, Mary."],["Gram","And I think he said it's costing the church $12,000."],["Gramps","Wow. Yes."],["Gram","What happened?"],["Gramps","Bill Supple got the last rites."],["Gram","Huh?"],["Gramps","I'll talk to you later about it."],["Mike","Hmm."],["Gramps","Bill Supple got the last rites."],["Gram","Huh?"],["Gramps","Bill Supple got the last rites!"],["Gram","Oh, Jesus. He's dying?"],["Gramps","Yeah. Well, if he got the last rites, I guess he is."],["Gram","Everybody we know is dying."],["Gramps","What was I up to now? Hmm."],["Mike","Uh, the Jersey Journal."],["Gramps","Okay. There's a local newspaper plant in Hoboken, the Jersey Journal. And the poor kids used to sell newspapers. And they used to go on a corner on the main street and, Jersey Journal, Jersey Journal! And they sold newspapers. Again, I wasn't allowed to sell newspapers, but I used to help the kids, okay? The newspapers used to— I think if you bought 2, it cost you 3 cents and you sold them for 2 cents each, whatever it was. It was cheap."],["Gram","Talk loudly. I want to hear what you did."],["Gramps","So I wasn't allowed to sell newspapers, but I used to help the kids sell newspapers. What else? What else? What else? Oh, growing up, we didn't have the luxury of the things they have today. Everything we did was in the streets because there was no- not that many cars. So any games we did was in the streets. We played box ball. Do you know what box ball is?"],["Mike","No."],["Gramps","Well, in Hoboken, the side streets were narrow. So what you did, you made home plate with chalk. You made home plate on one side. Up a little bit, you made first base with chalk mark. Across the other side of the street, you made second base. Down below, you made third base. Okay?"],["Mike","Yep."],["Gramps","So that was box ball. Okay? You had a pitcher, you had a first baseman, and maybe you had a third baseman, whatever it was. And you threw the ball in on a bounce and you slapped it. Okay?"],["Mike","With your hand?"],["Gramps","With your hand. You slapped the ball. They threw it in on a bounce. If you hit it so hard and it went over their head, it was an out. It had to stay on the ground. If they caught a fly ball infield, you were out. But anyway, that was the game we played. That was box ball. The other game you played in the street was stickball. You played with a broom handle. And the streets again were narrow, but now you had— the bases were longer. You had first base, second base was way down there, third base was here, and home was here. And I don't remember if they threw it on a pitch or on the ground. Again, you hit the ball. Okay? That was stickball. Again, there was not that much traffic. The other game in the streets was punchball. You punched the ball. Okay? Skating, you skated in the streets. You didn't go to roller skating rinks. You skated in the streets."],["Mike","Was it cobblestone or was it paved?"],["Gramps","No, no, it was paved. It was paved. Then you— if the skates started to get worn out a little bit, you converted them and you made scooters. For yourself. You got a case of some kind, I don't know what, maybe it was an orange crate or something, and you put it on a board and you put the wheels underneath. You had things and you went roller skating."],["Mike","Clever."],["Gramps","That was your skateboard. Okay? And I think that's about it. I can't think of anything else."]]},"20":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Mike","What's the story, it's not your story, but what's the one about where Papa, where they asked him Where he was working on the docks."],["Gramps","Oh, you want to hear that story? Yeah."],["Christine","Good story."],["Gramps","My father was a longshoreman. Well, he was actually— he started out as a longshoreman, then he became a hiring boss, which was a rank up, which was a great job. And as a rule, hiring bosses were on the take for everything, okay? My father was not that kind. For some reason, They picked him and he had nothing to do— he used to hire guys and they used to want— I remember they used to ring the doorbell."],["Gram","Part of this story, tell them, is when we went to visit. That's part of that."],["Gramps","Yeah. So they used to come and ring the doorbell and want to bring him wine and stuff. He'd never take it, okay? Because that wasn't his thing. He used to give them jobs because some guys couldn't get a job. And they were like part-time longshoremen. So one day, one day—."],["Gram","Sammy talked loud. Damn stupid hearing aid."],["Gramps","One day, for whatever reason, the guys that ran the docks, they were mobsters, okay? My father wasn't doing what they wanted him to do. So they got rid of him, okay? He couldn't get a job. They wouldn't stay on the piers anymore. So now he didn't work. I don't know how long he didn't work, but he used to— in those days, longshoremen, if they didn't have a regular group that they belonged to, they had to shape up and get picked. My father would go every day to shape up, and the guys that would pick and knew him, but they'd never pick him. And I don't know how long he went. But every day he'd go, but they'd never pick him. Even though they knew who he was, they wouldn't pick him. One day he's there and the guy says, you. He knew who he was. He says, you. You come in. My father says, me? The guy says, yeah, you. He says, you know what? I don't feel like working today. And he walked away. He wouldn't go back. But before he got fired from them, One of the guys that was a mobster and a Teamsters invited him up to his home up in someplace up in New York State. I forget where."],["Gram","What a house. We were keeping company."],["Gramps","His name was Polonetti. It was the Villa Polonetti."],["Mike","Nice."],["Gramps","Your mother was going— your grandmother was going with me. We weren't married yet."],["Gram","I was getting very nervous."],["Gramps","We went there. And this big house. My father got there and they were talking. But we didn't stay with them."],["Gram","I said to him, I'm getting out of here. It was like gangsters all over the place. What the hell am I getting into. We left."],["Gramps","No, no, no. That visit was okay. No, no. That visit was okay. You're talking about when we went to Brooklyn. If your name is Joseph, Joseph is a big holiday celebration if your name is Joseph."],["Gram","St. Joseph's."],["Gramps","His boss at that time was Joseph Polonetti. They had a big party for him in Brooklyn. We went, your grandmother and I, my mother and my father. We went to this big hall and there was a big crowd of people there. We were only there a little while and all of a sudden my father says, we're leaving. God knows what was going to happen and we left. We never found out. And I think that's when they got rid of him."],["Mike","Yup."],["Gram","Something was going on."],["Gramps","Something."],["Mike","That's scary."],["Gramps","But they were, they were, oh God. That's about the story."],["Gram","I'm starting to say, the hell kind of a family, am I getting it?"],["Mike","Yeah, no kidding."],["Gramps","Yeah, but that was my father. That's about it."]]},"21":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Mike","You got to tell the hat story. We got to get that straight from the horse's mouth."],["Gramps","Oh, you want that?"],["Gram","What's that?"],["Gramps","The hat story? Oh, that one. Okay. World War II had ended and my ship had just pulled into New Westminster, Canada, British Columbia. We just pulled in and our commanding officer announced that the war was over. Japan had surrendered. And so many of you guys can have liberty. They're having a big celebration in Vancouver, Canada, which was about maybe 15 or 20 miles from where our ship had pulled in. I was fortunate enough, I went. While we were celebrating in the streets, just like New York City, big celebration, it was fantastic, a kid grabs my hat and takes off. Okay? So I lost my hat. And I love my hat. Okay? I celebrated and I went back to the ship. And I was telling Michael the story and then Michael picked up the story—."],["Mike","Mm-hmm."],["Gramps","And put it on a website. Television station picked it up, newspaper picked it up, and I had my 15 minutes of fame on paper."],["Mike","But no hat."],["Gram","You were very fussy with your hat."],["Gramps","I liked my hat."],["Gram","The company, Louis, Betty's son, he was young. And every time he'd come in in his uniform, Louis would go for his hat. And he was so, don't touch my hat, you know. And then when the kid stole it, he was so upset."],["Gramps","The other story about the Navy, we're talking about dungarees, okay? What we used to do out at sea, while we were underway, we used to tie our dungarees on a heavy line, throw it overboard, and let it stay in the water as we were traveling along and kind of bleach the jeans, okay? And that's how we used to get them They didn't look so brand new all the time. The other thing, we didn't have ironing boards or irons. How we did our uniforms, our uniforms were pressed inside out. We used to lay them out underneath our mattress and sleep on them. Okay? Cause we had no ironing boards, except if we were in the States. But if we were overseas, that's how we ironed."],["Mike","Yeah."],["Gramps","And how we washed our blue uniforms, we had a bucket, Ivory soap with shavings, with water, with a toilet bowl plunger, and plunge our clothes in there. Okay? The white stripes on your uniform, you had a toothbrush, and with that same soap, You used to brush the white stripes. Okay? And that's the things we used to do."],["Mike","Jeez."],["Gramps","What else?"],["Gram","I was saying how Papa used to out-sit you every night when you'd visit."],["Gramps","When I was keeping company with your grandmother, I used to go every night to go see her and we'd sit in the kitchen and talk. Her father was there. Her mother was there."],["Gram","My father was so tired."],["Gramps","But her father had to get up early to go to work in the store."],["Gram","He used to get up at 3 o'clock."],["Gramps","But when I was there, he never wanted to go to bed. And I wasn't about ready to leave until he went to bed, because that would be my little time in the hallway with my girlfriend, okay? So I had to— he used to go upstairs, so I had a little time in the hallway with my girlfriend."],["Gram","So we could do some kissing."],["Gramps","That, that's about it. That's— I, I can't think of anything else."],["Gram","I can't believe where the years have gone. It doesn't seem possible."],["Mike","Not a bad life, neither of you."],["Gramps","All right, enough stories."],["Mike","Got some quality content?"],["Gramps","Huh?"],["Christine","I heard all my favorites."],["Mike","We heard all the greatest hits."]]},"22":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","One more story. Growing up, again growing up, we got our brand new— I was a kid— got our brand new radio. It was this cabinet console, whatever you called it. It was a Philco with a tilt front with the phonograph record. Equipment in there. And they only played 78s in those days. You know what a 78 is?"],["Mike F.","No."],["Gramps","Okay. A 78 was a disc, I'll show you one. It's a disc that if you dropped it, it would fall into a million pieces, okay? But anyway, and what the stores used to do if you bought a new Victrola, a phonograph, they'd give you one record. So the first record that they gave me was I still have it. I still have all my 78s. It was Dick Todd, he was a singer. And the first song that was on there was Along the Santa Fe Trail. Okay?"],["Mike F.","Okay."],["Gramps","That was my first record and that started me with the big bands and vocalists. Okay? And as a kid growing up I used to buy Billboard magazine, which used to tell you where the bands were playing. OK? And I used to see where they were playing, and on a penny postcard I used to write out and say, may I have your photograph please, the band leader or the singer? OK? And they used to send me. I had quite a collection of pictures from band leaders and singers."],["Gram","I think I know where this is going."],["Mike","I was wondering if this was going to come up. I didn't want to bring it up."],["Gramps","And I had made a nice album out of it. In fact, one day I was backstage, I used to go backstage in New York. We used to sneak over on a ferry boat, walk to the Paramount or the Strand, and see the big bands and the shows. I used to go like on a Saturday morning, and I think it was like a quarter. So I used to go over there and then after the show I used to go backstage waiting for them to come out and get their autograph, okay?"],["Mike","Mm-hmm."],["Gramps","So I had quite a collection. I had quite a collection of pictures. And I had them in a nice book, I did a nice job with them. In fact, one day even Sinatra came out and I got his autograph and a picture of him. And I had it in this book. I got married. And when I left for service, I told my mother, things you don't throw away on me. You don't throw away my records. You don't throw away my photo album. And you don't give away my bicycle. Okay? So when I came home, I had the bicycle. I had my records. And I had the photograph album. Now I get married, okay? And I had the photo and I put it in the closet. We didn't have a big closet. And it laid in the closet and it wasn't bothering anybody."],["Gram","Wait a minute. But you never told me."],["Mike F.","It wasn't bothering anybody."],["Gram","You never told me how much that meant to you."],["Gramps","It never bothered anybody in the closet. It wasn't taking up any room. No room at all. So one day I wanted to go find it and look at it. And I can't find it there. I says-"],["Gram","Oh shit."],["Gramps","Marietta, do you know what? You know where my book is? Uh uh uh. Do you know what happened? Where is it? I threw it away. WHAT? You threw it away?"],["Gram","We got married and had one closet."],["Gramps","But it didn't take up any room! It was a little book. How much room can I take in a closet?"],["Gram","I had no room to put. And I didn't realize, I really didn't know. He never mentioned this album to me."],["Gramps","If it laid there, it meant I wanted it. She threw it away."],["Gram","You never saw World War II."],["Gramps","Oh gosh."],["Gram","Oh god."],["Gramps","Yeah, that's all right. But I still have my 78s, every one I ever bought before and after the war."],["Christine","That's amazing."],["Mike","Do you still have your bicycle?"],["Gramps","No! We had it down the shore. I got pictures of it. Your grandmother had one. We ended up taking them down the shore, our bicycles. You had a bike."],["Gram","I don't know what happened to it."],["Gramps","Mine fell apart after a while."],["Gram","I had a bike. I had a nice one."],["Gramps","We had it down the shore."],["Gram","Yeah, but I don't remember."],["Gramps","They must have went. They were balloon tires, the big fat tires."],["Gram","Oh God. That was World War III."],["Mike","I love it. He still gets worked up every time that story comes up."],["Gramps","I wasn't gonna mention it. I forgot all about it."],["Mike","It was on my list, but I wasn't gonna touch it."],["Gramps","Michael. It was a shape like this, maybe this big. How much room could that take in a closet?"],["Gram","It was an album. Well, in Hoboken, we needed every inch of space we had."],["Gramps","But anyway."],["Gram","When Conrad was born, I needed room. This book is laying up here. Oh, I'll get rid of it."]]},"23":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gramps","You want to hear another story?"],["Gram","Yeah."],["Gramps","Your father."],["Mike","Yeah, we haven't had any stories about Dad."],["Christine","Yeah."],["Gramps","When, when Conrad was little, we made him take accordion lessons. Okay? Now, he wasn't too thrilled with it, but he used to practice."],["Mike","Hard to believe."],["Mike F.","I can't imagine why."],["Gramps","But, but, but my thought was not from so much with the accordion. At one time they came out, it was like a piano, it was the accordion board laid out like a piano. And I figured, gee, that would be nice. It had everything just like the accordion except it was like a piano. And I figured if he learns that, he can go into that, you know, instead of having this thing in it. But he didn't like it."],["Mike","Can't think of my dad with an accordion."],["Gramps","So one day we're down the shore, we're down my my in-law's place, her mother's place. The garage door. Conrad, he wouldn't practice his accordion, okay?"],["Gram","No, he was fighting with Sergio or something. They were having an argument."],["Gramps","Wasn't the accordion?"],["Gram","No, it wasn't because—."],["Gramps","Well, he was doing something wrong that day."],["Gram","Gert was there. She never saw-"],["Gramps","I never hit Conrad. I have to tell you. When Conrad misbehaved. I remember one time I took my belt off. I didn't want to hit him. I wanted to scare him, but I accidentally hit him on the leg. So I said I'll never do that again. But this one day he was acting up, him and Sergio, and we were down in the garage and they had an aluminum door, garage door. So if you hit it, it made a lot of noise. I got him, I put him up against the door, and I kicked the bottom of it. And it made a lot of noise and they thought I was beating him up. And he's, gosh, shaking. And they said, what are you doing? I said, I don't know. That was it."],["Gram","Gert always thinks of that. She said it sounded like he was beating the hell out of him. He never touched him."],["Mike","Putting the fear of God into him."],["Gramps","Now, years ago, we used to do our own painting, okay?"],["Gram","Oh, tell that story. That's the story."],["Gramps","Did our own painting. Now, that was before Rollers came out."],["Mike F.","Okay."],["Gramps","Now we're painting an apartment with 5 rooms, whatever it is."],["Mike F.","Wow."],["Gramps","And with a brush."],["Mike F.","Oh God."],["Gramps","Not water paint, oil paint. Okay? Ceiling and all that. And we used to paint because Hoboken wasn't the cleanest town."],["Gram","First we had the— we decided to put Sanitas, and you had to give maybe 4 coats. I had, wWhen we first got married, we had in the dining room, we had gray Sanitas. That was my Aunt Beatrice's love, Sanitas. And it had dark red maroon plumes in it. In the living room, we had green plumes. I felt like I was in a jungle. So I decided I wanted my rooms painted. So do you know how many coats he had to give to cover all that?"],["Mike","Oh my God."],["Gram","So, Gert and Janie, Janie had taken the apartment downstairs."],["Gramps","Downstairs. Right."],["Gram","And that's why they were there when this happened."],["Gramps","But we used to use paintbrushes. Then the rollers came out. And when I was working, one of the guys working said, Sam, use the rollers, they're great. I said, I don't know. So I said, okay. So the rollers were great. So I was painting the dining room. I was painting the ceiling. And that table sat in the middle of the room just like it is there. And I had pushed it all the way into the corner."],["Gram","And it had a glass—."],["Gramps","The Italians years ago, you had to have glass tops on your tables. God forbid you didn't have a glass top on your table."],["Mike","And plastic on the furniture."],["Gram","Glass to cover the stain in your table."],["Gramps","So I had pushed it all the way to the corner, just like this corner here. And I'm painting. And then I was near the end and I said, I'm not gonna move. I'll stand on the table. Okay? So I'm painting and boom, the table breaks. The whole front goes down. I go down, the glass top all around me, and I'm laying over here. She was— they were downstairs. They come running up and I'm laying. I'm not moving because I see all these big—."],["Gram","All broken. I was downstairs. The 3 of us went running."],["Gramps","Glass."],["Gram","Janie Gert and I come running up. And we see him laying in the middle of all this broken glass."],["Mike","Covered in paint."],["Gramps","Oh yeah, oh yeah, the paint went flying."],["Gram","And we said, Sammy! Are you all right? When are we going to eat, I'm hungry. I never forgot that. And the leg of that table went into that piece of furniture and it like did damage to the door. But trying to get him out of the all this broken glass—."],["Gramps","I said, don't move, be careful. All I was thinking was one of these things will really grab me. I was lucky."],["Gram","Oh my, I'm hungry. When are we going to eat?"],["Gramps","I figured it was my last meal."],["Mike","What about the scaffolding? When I think when you were pregnant with Dad."],["Gram","The what?"],["Gramps","Oh, the scaffolding?"],["Mike","You were doing something outside of the house and—."],["Gramps","Oh, you fell."],["Gram","He would come flying past me."],["Gramps","What were we doing? I forgot."],["Gram","They were working on the back of the house."],["Gramps","I forget that."],["Gram","I was pregnant. How I didn't have a miscarriage, I'll never know. And I was— yeah, you even just mentioned it not long ago."],["Gramps","I forget that."],["Gram","And I don't know if the scaffold tipped or whatever, but I happened to be by the window and I see him come flying past. Thank God. I don't know how he didn't get killed, honest to God."],["Gramps","And across the street where she was born and her parents lived, we had a shed, shed in the back. Okay?"],["Gram","Oh, Jesus."],["Mike","You can see where Dad gets his handiness from."],["Gramps","So they decided they're going to knock this down. It was wood. And they're going to build it out of blocks. So I'm there, Tommy, her brother-in-law, and Charlie, Angela's husband at that time. We're tearing it around, we're taking it apart a little bit at a piece. And I'm, I'm in the middle there, and I'm saying to myself, we're wasting so much time doing this. So I figured I'll pull one whole thing down, it'll all come down. The whole thing come down right on my head. Thank God where it came down was open. It came right down around, it came down."],["Gram","When they built the garage down the shore, they said, don't touch nothing. That was all cinder blocks. Oh my God. He's dangerous. He's dangerous."],["Gramps","That's it. I don't think there's anything else."],["Gram","And then when I was pregnant—."],["Gramps","Oh, she's got stories."],["Gram","I couldn't— in the baby's room, which had been Conrad's room, the one window was— outside of it was a fire escape. So usually I used to climb out onto the fire escape to wash the outside. But now I'm 9 months pregnant and I couldn't climb out. So I said to him, Sam, would you just go outside and wash that one? He never washed windows in his life."],["Gramps","I made up for that."],["Gram","I'm in the kitchen, he goes out, I hear crash. He rubbed the window so hard he broke the glass. He says, I do that on purpose so you don't ask me to do anything."],["Gramps","I never washed windows."],["Gram","That was the last one you had watched."],["Gramps","Yeah. Well, I can't think of anything else. I think we talked long enough."]]},"24":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gram","And then when I had Conrad."],["Mike","She's still going."],["Gram","At the back of my mother's house-"],["Gramps","Michael, this is good for her."],["Gram","-which we lived with my mother for a while to get the apartment, they could see the labor room at the hospital. And they kept watching because I went into labor on my birthday. And, uh, they— the doctor, we got in touch with the doctor and he kept telling us what to do. And finally he said, all right, get her to the hospital. So it was like morning. So he, he would come. And I loved plums. That was my longing. I loved— the one thing I loved was plums. So I was in the labor room that day and He'd come, and I said, Sam, bring me plums. And it was funny, I wasn't having— I wasn't ready, even though my water had broke. I had said to the nurse, how do you know when the baby is ready to come out? She said, we feel the head. So I kept feeling myself to see if I could feel the head. But anyway, before that happened, he came in. Just as he's going to give me the plums, I got this one horrible pain. I took the plums and I threw them at him. Remember?"],["Gramps","I remember."],["Gram","And then finally I felt the head and I started scre— no, then you left."],["Gramps","Oh yeah, of course I left."],["Mike","He got the message."],["Gram","Well, looking out the window and all of a sudden he said they saw a lot of activity."],["Mike","That's so cool."],["Christine","That's really neat."],["Gram","They came running. He came running, and I had felt the head, and I start screaming, nurse! Nurse! My baby's coming out! That's when you guys saw."],["Gramps","Well, we could see you."],["Gram","When Conrad was born."],["Gramps","That room happened to be just—."],["Christine","That's amazing."],["Mike","So cool."],["Christine","Did you hold up a blue blanket for the boy? That would have been kind of cool"],["Mike","They're out there with binoculars."],["Gram","The doctor said, if you don't have the baby by 7 o'clock, we're gonna have to do a cesarean. He wouldn't turn. But then all of a sudden he turned and was coming out. But oh God. And in those days you didn't know what you were going to have anyway. But he was beautiful. He was a beautiful baby. Oh God."],["Christine","He's okay."],["Mike","Didn't last long."],["Gramps","And the guy that took the pictures, in those days you'd post it in the newspaper, and this photographer, he was a Latino, I remember that."],["Mike","Is this the guy that came to the door? Or is this in the hospital?"],["Gramps","No, at the door at home. Of course, you used to put a notice in the paper."],["Mike","Right, right. Yup."],["Gramps","And I guess these guys used to follow that. He came, Michael. He took him on our kitchen table, put a screen behind him, and he took pictures. They were beautiful. You could buy as many as you want. We bought all of them. They came out so good."],["Gram","There's one in that guest bedroom."],["Gramps","Yeah."],["Gram","I love that picture."],["Mike","That's from that guy?"],["Gramps","Oh yeah, he was good. I don't know what his name was, but that's how they used to get jobs. They used to see your ads in the paper. It was good."],["Mike","Smart."],["Gram","We bought them all because one was more beautiful than the other."],["Gramps","So that's our stories."],["Mike","Pretty good stories. I think we hit on everything."],["Gramps","A little crazy, Mike."],["Gram","When I was pregnant, we used to take the mattress off our bed because it was our bedroom and then there was the living room, and we used to carry it under the windows."],["Christine","Oh."],["Gram","Because it was so hot. We didn't have air conditioning in those days. Remember? We used to sleep under the window."],["Gramps","Yeah."],["Gram","Oh, God. Mike, you heard stories that you'll never forget."],["Mike F.","I think that's the point."],["Mike","That's why we're here."],["Gram","God. Dear God."],["Mike","This is good. Those were good stories."],["Gramps","Where is all that gone, Mike?"],["Gram","Where— we had a—."],["Mike F.","I got it all in this computer here."],["Gramps","Holy mackerel."]]},"25":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gram","I remember that woman that stopped us, huh, when we came out of Burger King that day."],["Mike","Definitely got it."],["Gram","This woman  She stopped us when we were coming out of Burger King."],["Gramps","She was a Latino. She was a Latina."],["Gram","She said, I want to talk to you. We were leaving. She said, you're such a cute couple."],["Gramps","Yeah, we're a cute couple."],["Gram","Yes, that's our name. She'd evidently seen us in there a few times. We never— she said, please, can I take— she took our picture. Took our picture. She isn't the only one. You've got to be careful."],["Gramps","And sometimes they say, how long you been married? How long you been married? We say, 71 years. Oh, that's wonderful. I say, yeah, but next month we're thinking of getting divorced. And sometimes they say—."],["Gram","Twice."],["Gramps","How long you guys been together? Oh, I say, 71 years. And next month we're thinking of getting married."],["Mike F.","We're starting to get serious."],["Gramps","I told I told I told that about getting divorced to a woman at my church. I thought she was going to have a nervous breakdown. I said, \"I'm only kidding. I'm only kidding.\" Remember that woman? How she got so nervous and upset? I said, \"I'm only kidding.\""],["Mike F.","You should say we just met."],["Mike","Match.com."],["Gramps","And you know what's the other nice thing? I got that hat that says U.S. World War II, U.S. Navy. Yeah. And I wear it. And I like when the young— especially the young people, when they say, thank you for your service. I really. When the young people say, I really appreciate it."],["Mike","Good."],["Gram","Remember that little boy who said it?"],["Gramps","Yeah, well, his father must have told him."],["Gram","His father said it and then he said it."],["Gramps","But you know what? We're in IHOP. This has happened about 4 times now. We're in IHOP and I'm waiting for my bill and the waitress— we got to know the waitress there, Christine. We don't even order there. She knows what we want. She automatically orders it."],["Mike","Of course."],["Gramps","She says, uh, your bill is paid for. I said, really?"],["Mike","That's amazing."],["Gramps","She says, the gentleman picked it up."],["Gram","It's happened about 4 or 5 times."],["Gramps","Now the bill is like $25."],["Gram","I say to him, wear your hat every time."],["Mike","Yeah, everywhere you go."],["Gramps","I think it's happened 3 times in IHOP. It's happened a couple of times in Burger King."],["Mike","Never pay full price. Nice"],["Gramps","And it happened in Applebee's."],["Gram","Oh, that was $38."],["Mike","Wow."],["Gram","That was a couple of—."],["Gramps","She said, make sure you wear your hat all the time."],["Mike F.","You gotta order a couple drinks."],["Gramps","Oh boy, I'm telling you."],["Gram","Guy in Burger King was going out, he was leaving, and he threw something on the table. Threw a $25 gift card."],["Christine","Nice."],["Gram","On the table."],["Mike","That's so cool."],["Gram","And he was gone."],["Gramps","And he took right off. I couldn't even say thank you."],["Mike","Just throwing money at you."],["Gramps","Yeah, I'm telling you, it's amazing. I tell you, I really— with the young kids, I really appreciate it because I don't even know what they teach in school about it anymore."],["Mike","Yeah."],["Gramps","But when a young person says it, I really appreciate it. And I like it when another guy that's been in service thanks me and they tell me they were in Vietnam. So what I tell them, I said, you guys weren't recognized. At least we were recognized. You guys got recognized long after. But to thank me, I mean, you were there too. Your time was even worse than mine."],["Mike","There was a group when I went to Dunkin this morning of 5 or 6 guys, and I saw the hats and it looked similar to yours. I was trying to see, but they were younger. And then I saw they were all Vietnam. Dunkin."],["Gramps","Dunkin? Dunkin, yeah."],["Gram","He had a hat, the same one that, you know, I don't know where you got. I think you sent away for—."],["Gramps","That's from my organization. Yeah."],["Gram","Every time he wore it, he looked so ridiculous. It's a big hat, like, you know, it's a baseball hat, but he looked so stupid in it. And I'd always say to him, don't wear it."],["Gramps","Are you taping that, Mike?"],["Mike F.","Of course I am."],["Gramps","Oh God."],["Gram","A couple of years ago, I called your father on a sneak, you know, and I said, Conrad, do you know anybody who makes baseball hats? Your father can get me anything. He said yes."],["Mike","Magic box. Yes."],["Gram","So I had written everything down and I told him what I wanted and he got the hat. And then I said that was a wonderful Christmas gift for him, you know. Yeah. So I don't care that you wear it. He used to get mad. You never meant for me to wear my hat. I said, you look so stupid in it. I don't want you to wear it. But this hat I don't want you to take off."],["Mike","Yeah, it's a good— it's a gift that keeps on giving."],["Christine","Exactly."],["Gram","Yeah. Hey, I think the hat cost $30. $30."],["Mike","Paid for itself and then some."],["Gram","And I— he always, when he heard it was a lot of money, I said, Jesus, it's paid for itself. Yeah, time and time again."],["Mike F.","At least in Burger King gift cards."],["Mike","Yeah"],["Gram","That's, that's nice."],["Gramps","See a side of her you never saw before."],["Mike","I know, aggressive, violent grandmother."],["Gram","Uncle Tommy. He was in the Army, and he always told a story. He was married and Louis was a baby when he went away. That was tough. He always told the story. They were in a foxhole and they were scared to death. Where was he?"],["Gramps","Germany. I don't know what part of Germany."],["Gram","All of a sudden they saw something moving, and they all started shooting like crazy. It was the rising sun. But they got so scared—."],["Gramps","Not the Japanese, the rising sun."],["Mike","Right. The actual sun."],["Gram","They always told that story. They start shooting like crazy. It was a rough time for those guys. He was in a while, and it was hard. Louis was only a little baby when he went away. Oh God, Mike, you made This Is Your Life very important. See that?"],["Mike F.","What's that? I got the headphones on, I can—."],["Gram","You don't know what a crazy—."],["Gramps","She's never talked that much before. My God, how many more stories you're gonna tell?"],["Gram","Now you know what a crazy family you got into."],["Mike F.","Yeah, well, I think we got into the best kind of family."],["Gramps","Oh gosh. That was interesting. Very good."],["Mike","That was fun. Thank you, guys."],["Gramps","Thank you. Thank you."],["Mike","Thank you for coming so prepared with your stories."],["Gramps","That's not my list, that's her list. And she added to it."],["Gram","Oh God, I was trying to remember things that happened."]]},"26":{"note":"Transcript text is preserved from the reviewed master transcript. Speaker labels are normalized for display.","lines":[["Gram","I forgot about the Teamsters and all that. But I was good at pulling teeth. Dr. Peluso hated him. What did he call you yet?"],["Gramps","I don't know. I don't want to— I can't remember."],["Gram","He want— they had no children, and they more or less adopted me."],["Mike","Yeah."],["Gram","Because I was only right out of high school when I went to work for him. And I wanted to be a dentist. I just loved it. And he would have put me through dental school. They were ready to do it. And then I started going with Grandpa, and he always said, He ruined your life. But he was a drinker, my boss."],["Mike","Oh, drunken dentist."],["Gram","We had a basement apartment downstairs, he had, and I used to make him get down there and lock him in. And then I used to call up his nephew, Jack and Pete. They'd come because I'd say, he can't work, I gotta get him out of here. They'd take him home with their car, so he didn't have a car. He'd take a taxi and come back. Oh, he was really bad news."],["Gramps","Hey, that's an addiction."],["Gram","One day he was drinking, and you know, he'd give me a hard time. I mean, I'm telling him, you can't work."],["Mike","Yeah."],["Gram","Yeah, a little girl. She had to have a tooth pulled. She was with her grandmother. We had an alcove where the parents would sit and the patient would go in. So he injected the little girl. He did that good. Then when I got the kid in to have the tooth pulled, He's starting to go to the other side."],["Mike F.","Oh, Jesus."],["Gram","That's the liquor. I grabbed his hand, and I got the kid out of the chair. And he knew right away that what I did was right. And I got the kid out, so now I got to tell the grandmother something. So I said, he can't pull it today because she's got a little abscess there, so you have to come back, you know. Let him rinse and made up a story."],["Mike","Smart. Clever."],["Gram","He was ready to pull the kid's tooth on the other. And that's— I quit. He came back from lunch that day. Oh, I worked for him a long time. I was due to go on vacation and I was getting 2 weeks' pay that was due me for my vacation. But he was cockeyed drunk. He was down in the basement. We had Isabel working with us. She was there about a year, and she knew about his drinking. So I went downstairs and I said, Doctor, I'm going to go on vacation. Do you want to give me my check? You don't deserve a check. I mean, he was cockeye drunk. I tried to get paid, but But he wasn't going to give me it. I said, you know what? Shove it!"],["Gram","I went upstairs and Isabel heard me yelling at him. She says, you quit? I'm leaving too. She says, I'm not staying here without you. So I went in the drawer. Patients had paid cash. I took all the cash that was due me."],["Mike","Yeah."],["Gram","I wasn't going to get screwed."],["Gramps","Good for you."],["Gram","I took that. That was my—."],["Mike","Made that abundantly clear today."],["Gram","But I always feel very bad because this was like at 3 o'clock in the afternoon. The patients are going to start coming. Isabelle and I just walked out. I should have called his nephew. I often wonder what happened when these patients came. You know, the girls, we weren't there. He was down there. I often wonder what happened."],["Gramps","You'll never know."],["Gram","Then he hired somebody after, which I found out through his nephews. One of his nephews was an upholsterer. He did all my upholstery and everything. They were great guys. And he hired a woman that was married, but she must have been a tramp, and she knew what— she knew about his drinking. Because his wife was gorgeous, Mrs. Peluso, but she drank just as much as him. She had a diamond ring that would knock your eyes out. It was gorgeous, and all kinds of furs. This girl, from what I heard from the nephews, got everything. So God knows what she did with it."],["Gramps","She wiped him out."],["Gram","Took his. And then they found her dead on the floor, the wife. And that's how they found him dead one day on the floor."],["Mike F.","Oh."],["Gram","They drank themselves to death. They had no children."],["Gramps","And what's the other story you could tell them? When you used to visit them down the shore. Oh, bless them."],["Gram","They drank and drank. They had a bungalow. It was a house."],["Gramps","In Brick."],["Gram","We went to Sea-Wood Park and Eagle Point."],["Gramps","Eagle Point. That was the exclusive part."],["Gram","That was, yeah."],["Gramps","All the professionals lived there. Where they lived were the poor people."],["Gram","He had a yacht. And he loved to take you out on his yacht, but he'd drink. I'd never want to go on his yacht. But we went to visit him, you and me, and of course out comes the drinks. And we don't drink. I mean, so I'm sitting next to this potted plant, so I get a drink. That plant between the two of us got more drinks in it that day. It must have grown."],["Gramps","Well, you know, as much as they had money, when he entertained certain people, they were Scotch drinkers. Chivas Regal was the classic."],["Gram","Oh, yeah, yeah."],["Gramps","He'd buy the cheapest scotch and put it in the Chivas Regal bottle. Okay? And that's what he used to do."],["Mike","Classy."],["Gramps","But if he was drinking it straight, he'd have his own Chivas Regal. But if he was serving company, he used that."],["Mike","You get the cheap stuff."],["Gramps","Of course they were mixing it. Yeah."],["Gram","And I guess— I had my office was here. Look, there was a door in my office and it was like a little bit of an alcove where he had a desk and he'd go in there to make out his checks and stuff. But there was a shelf. I never paid attention. One day I was straightening up. That's when the bottles— I'd say to myself, how come he's getting tipsy? He had more liquor back there. He'd go in there and take a swig. But the whiskey. Oh, like he said. And then, then we saw that he was—."],["Gramps","Yeah, he used to buy ShopRite scotch. Yeah, that's what he used to do. But if he was drinking it himself, he used the Chivas Regal. Did you ever use that Chivas Regal I gave you?"],["Mike","Uh, not yet."],["Gramps","Well, don't let it go to waste."],["Mike","No, I'm keeping that for me, and then I have the other bottle I pour for the guests."],["Gramps","I think that's about $40 a bottle now. We paid $5 in Bermuda."],["Gram","We got rid of a lot. We got rid of a lot."],["Gramps","All right, I think, I think we've talked enough, kiddo."],["Gram","Can I give you something to eat?"],["Christine","No."],["Mike","Yeah. Yeah, I'm not hungry."],["Christine","That Burger King sits with you."],["Gramps","You got enough for your gigs? I mean, I'm sure you're gonna use some of this, but you know what? We got this copyrighted, you can't use it."],["Gram","You could use it all."],["Gramps","Oh, she likes it. Uh, oh Mike, that, that,"],["Mike","That was fun. Thank you guys."],["Gramps","In other words, that's been picking up us just then and that picks it up?"],["Mike F.","Uh, yeah, everything."],["Gramps","But, but you control that though from there, Mike?"],["Mike F.","Uh, yeah, the volume from this little box here. Yeah, and then it picks up everything in the middle of the The table that we've been talking about."],["Gramps","Oh gosh, it's amazing."]]}};
